# PyCharm
#  JetBrains specific template is maintained in a separate JetBrains.gitignore that can
#  be found at https://github.com/github/gitignore/blob/main/Global/JetBrains.gitignore
#  and can be added to the global gitignore or merged into this file.  For a more nuclear
#  option (not recommended) you can uncomment the following to ignore the entire idea folder.
#.idea/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Survey data and secrets (requests-to-twilio specific)
#
# This project reads phone numbers and writes decrypted survey responses.
# None of that may ever reach the repository - it is Confidential under IPA's
# data classification and this repo is public.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Secrets
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.pem
*.key
service-account*.json

# Encryption private keys. `rtt keygen` writes rtt_private_key.txt by default,
# but --out can put it anywhere, so match the shape broadly. Losing control of
# this file exposes every response ever encrypted to its public key.
rtt_private_key*
*private_key*.txt
*private-key*.txt

# Local Claude Code settings. `.mcp.json` is shared and must stay free of
# secrets; the Twilio API key/secret it references live here instead.
.claude/settings.local.json

# Inputs and outputs containing respondent data
*_output.xlsx
*_output.csv
*_decrypted.csv
data/
input/
output/

# Everything spreadsheet-shaped, except the checked-in example. sample_input.xlsx
# is the only spreadsheet that may ever be committed; it uses the +1 555
# 0100-0199 range, which is reserved for fiction and is not routable.
*.xlsx
*.xls
*.csv
!sample_input.xlsx

# The generated value labels. These are the opposite of respondent data - they
# are what makes a column of codes readable, and they must ship with the
# instrument or the dataset cannot be interpreted. Same trailing /* reason as
# flows/ above: a bare `codebook/` would make the negation dead.
!codebook/*.csv

# Pulled flow definitions are not tracked. They are snapshots of state that
# lives in Twilio - `just flow-pull <name>` re-fetches any of them - and they
# are large enough to bury a real change in a diff.
#
# Three are deliberate exceptions, because they are the worked example this repo
# ships rather than snapshots of someone's live survey. All three are the same
# A/B demonstration of how question format changes WhatsApp survey answers,
# built for conference sessions, not study instruments - teaching material,
# which is what a public template needs.
#
#   data_use_demo_en / _es  The maintained pair, both generated by
#                           `just build-demo-flow` from one structure and two
#                           string tables. They are tracked despite being
#                           generated because the pair IS the lesson: the same
#                           instrument in two languages, side by side, is what
#                           somebody starting their own round should read
#                           first. Reviewing the diff is also how a change to
#                           the shared graph gets checked against both.
#
#   foro_nacional_datos     The as-ran original from the Foro Nacional de Datos
#                           in Bogota. Kept unmodified as provenance: it is the
#                           version that actually collected data, and it cannot
#                           be regenerated here anyway - it came from a
#                           different Twilio account, where its templates and
#                           Functions services 404.
# NOTE the trailing /* rather than a bare `flows/`. Git cannot re-include a file
# inside an excluded *directory*, so `flows/` would make every negation below
# silently dead - the files simply never appear in `git status`, which is how
# this was originally written and why it took a while to notice.
flows/*
!flows/data_use_demo_en.json
# Forward-looking, not a mistake: the Spanish flow builds from the same
# structure, but its two bookend templates do not exist on this account yet, so
# `just build-demo-flow --lang es` stops and says which to create. When somebody
# does, the built flow belongs beside the English one rather than ignored.
!flows/data_use_demo_es.json
!flows/foro_nacional_datos_source.json
